@*
    What a show did, once it has been run. One component, used by both the career screen and
    the exhibition one.

    **There were two of these.** `BookShowScreen` reports a career date and `ShowScreen`
    reports a sandbox show, so each grew its own copy of the result markup — and the copies
    then drifted, in both directions, until each one could report things the other could not:

      • A battle royal booked in a *career* reported nothing at all. The eliminations and the
        iron man were rendered only by the exhibition screen.
      • A career show never said which item took the fatigue penalty, or what the position
        weighting did to a score — the two lines that explain why a match scored lower than it
        was worked. Exhibition had both.
      • Exhibition never showed injuries, championship changes, or the brand report.
      • Exhibition rendered the status and feud panels unconditionally, so a show where
        nothing moved got two empty headings.

    That is what duplicated markup costs: not the second copy, but the third thing somebody
    adds to one of them.

    **Nothing here is switched on a mode.** Every section is gated on whether the result
    actually contains it — a brand report exists or it does not, a card item is a rumble or it
    is not — so there is no flag saying "career" for the next section to forget to check. What
    genuinely differs between the two screens is the heading above this and the buttons below
    it, and those stay on the screens.
*@
@using WrestlingSim.Models.Rumble

<div class="score-hero">
    <div>
        <div class="score-hero__label">Overall</div>
        <div class="score-hero__main @Grades.OfScore(Result.OverallRating).Tone()">@Result.OverallRating.ToString("F1") <span class="muted">/ 100</span></div>
    </div>
    <div>
        <div class="score-hero__label">Runtime</div>
        <div class="score-hero__main">@Result.BookedMinutes<span class="muted"> / @Result.BudgetMinutes min</span></div>
    </div>
    <div>
        <div class="score-hero__label">Final crowd</div>
        <div class="score-hero__main">@Result.FinalCrowdMood.ToString("F1") <span class="muted">/ 10</span></div>
    </div>
</div>

@if (Result.OverrunPenalty > 0)
{
    <div class="notice notice--error mt">
        <strong>The show ran long.</strong> Overall score cut by
        @((Result.OverrunPenalty * 100).ToString("F0"))%.
    </div>
}

@* Above the card. A rating is a number; somebody being out for six months is the thing that
   changes next month's booking. *@
@if (Result.Injuries.Count > 0)
{
    <div class="notice notice--error mt">
        <strong>Hurt tonight.</strong>
        @foreach (var hurt in Result.Injuries)
        {
            <div>
                @hurt.Wrestler.RingName — @hurt.Injury.Reason(Today)
                @if (hurt.Repeat) { <text> · not the first time</text> }
            </div>
        }
    </div>
}

@* The bill for the night's crossovers, presented after the rating they bought.
   docs/wrestling-reference/22-brand-splits.md §4.1. Null on a company-wide show, on an
   unsplit promotion, and in exhibition — so it is gated on the data rather than on the mode. *@
@if (Result.Brand is { } brandReport)
{
    <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel__title">
            @brandReport.BrandName
            <span class="spacer"></span>
            <span class="small muted">
                Split integrity @brandReport.IntegrityBefore.ToString("F1")
                → @brandReport.IntegrityAfter.ToString("F1")
            </span>
        </div>

        @if (brandReport.Crossovers.Count == 0)
        {
            <div class="notice notice--info">
                Kept to its own roster. Worth
                +@((brandReport.ExclusivityBonus * 100).ToString("F1"))% on the night, and it
                is worth that only for as long as the split means something.
            </div>
        }
        else
        {
            <div class="notice notice--warn">
                <strong>
                    @brandReport.Crossovers.Count crossover@(brandReport.Crossovers.Count == 1 ? "" : "s")
                    — split integrity down
                    @((brandReport.IntegrityBefore - brandReport.IntegrityAfter).ToString("F1")),
                    ceiling now @brandReport.Ceiling.ToString("F1").
                </strong>
                <ul>
                    @foreach (var crossover in brandReport.Crossovers)
                    {
                        <li>
                            @crossover.Wrestler (@crossover.HomeBrand) —
                            worth +@((crossover.Attraction * 100).ToString("F0"))% on the items they
                            worked, cost @crossover.Cost.ToString("F1") integrity, of which
                            @((crossover.Cost * BrandIntegrity.PermanentShare).ToString("F1")) does not come back.
                        </li>
                    }
                </ul>
            </div>
        }

        @if (brandReport.StarMakingFactor < 0.995)
        {
            <div class="notice notice--warn">
                Winners on this show kept
                @((brandReport.StarMakingFactor * 100).ToString("F0"))% of the standing they earned.
                A brand that does not mean anything cannot make anybody.
            </div>
        }
    </div>
}

<div class="panel">
    <div class="panel__title">Card</div>
    @for (int i = 0; i < Result.Items.Count; i++)
    {
        @* Counted, not `IndexOf`. Both copies searched the list for the item to number it —
           correct, since these are reference-equal instances, but a scan per row to learn
           something the loop already knows. *@
        var item = Result.Items[i];
        <div class="editor-row @(item.FatiguePenaltyApplied ? "is-flagged" : "")">
            <div class="editor-row__n">@(i + 1)</div>
            <div>
                <div class="editor-row__name">@item.Label</div>
                @* Why it scored what it scored. The position weighting and the fatigue
                   penalty are the two reasons a well-worked match comes out low, and the
                   career screen used to show neither. *@
                <div class="editor-row__sub">
                    @item.Kind · @item.DurationMinutes min · position ×@item.PositionWeight.ToString("0.0")
                    @if (item.FatiguePenaltyApplied)
                    {
                        <text> · fatigue ×0.85</text>
                    }
                </div>
                @foreach (var note in item.Notes)
                {
                    <div class="editor-row__sub">@note</div>
                }
            </div>
            <div class="editor-row__tools">
                @if (item.StarRating is { } stars)
                {
                    <Stars Rating="@stars" ShowNumber="false" />
                }
                <strong class="tabnum @Grades.OfScore(item.Score).Tone()">@item.Score.ToString("F1")</strong>
            </div>
        </div>
    }
</div>

@* Status movement, feud movement and play-by-play exist only for a show run this session — a
   reloaded save keeps the result summary, not the beat-by-beat detail. Injuries above are the
   exception that matters: those are written onto the wrestler, so a reloaded save still knows
   who is out even when these blocks have gone. Which is also why each is gated on its own
   count: a reloaded show would otherwise render three empty headings. *@
@if (Result.TitleUpdates.Count > 0)
{
    <TitleChanges Updates="@Result.TitleUpdates" />
}

@if (Result.StatusChanges.Count > 0)
{
    <StatusChanges Changes="@Result.StatusChanges" />
}

@if (Result.FeudUpdates.Count > 0)
{
    <FeudUpdates Updates="@Result.FeudUpdates" />
}

@foreach (var item in Result.Items.Where(i => i.MatchResult is not null))
{
    <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel__title">@item.Label — play by play</div>
        <PlayByPlay Result="item.MatchResult!" />
    </div>
}

@* A battle royal reports differently because it is graded differently — no play by play,
   because there are no beats, and the eliminations and the iron man instead. *@
@foreach (var item in Result.Items.Where(i => i.RumbleResult is not null))
{
    var rumble = item.RumbleResult!;
    <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel__title">@item.Label</div>

        @if (rumble.IronMan is { } iron && rumble.IronManOutlasted > 0 && iron != rumble.Winner)
        {
            <div class="notice notice--info">
                <strong>@iron.RingName</strong> outlasted @rumble.IronManOutlasted of them
                without winning it — the format's other rub, and a real one.
            </div>
        }

        <div class="stack">
            @foreach (var e in rumble.Eliminations)
            {
                <div class="beat-row">
                    <div class="beat-row__n">@e.Order</div>
                    <div class="beat-row__body">
                        <span class="beat-row__name">@e.Wrestler.RingName</span>
                        <span class="beat-row__sub">
                            @(e.By is null ? "in the pile-up" : $"by {e.By.RingName}")
                            · @e.Remaining left
                        </span>
                    </div>
                </div>
            }
            <div class="beat-row is-finish">
                <div class="beat-row__n">★</div>
                <div class="beat-row__body">
                    <span class="beat-row__name">@rumble.Winner.RingName</span>
                    <span class="beat-row__sub">last one standing</span>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
}

@* A drawing has no beats and no eliminations, so what it reports is what was said and what
   the numbers came out as — plus how much of the drum anybody believed. *@
@foreach (var item in Result.Items.Where(i => i.DrawResult is not null))
{
    var draw = item.DrawResult!;
    <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel__title">
            @item.Label
            <span class="spacer"></span>
            <span class="small muted">
                numbers @((draw.Reaction * 100).ToString("F0"))% ·
                believed @((draw.Credibility * 100).ToString("F0"))%
            </span>
        </div>

        <div class="stack">
            @foreach (var pull in draw.Pulls)
            {
                <div class="beat-row @(pull.Rigged ? "is-flagged" : "")">
                    <div class="beat-row__n">@pull.Number</div>
                    <div class="beat-row__body">
                        <span class="beat-row__name">@pull.Wrestler.RingName</span>
                        <span class="beat-row__sub">
                            @(pull.Rigged ? "handed it" : "drew it")
                            · worth @((pull.Reaction * 100).ToString("F0"))%
                        </span>
                    </div>
                </div>
            }
        </div>

        <div class="mt">
            @foreach (var line in draw.Commentary)
            {
                <div class="pbp__line">@line</div>
            }
        </div>
    </div>
}

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ShowResult Result { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>
    /// The date the show ran, which is what turns an injury into "out 30 weeks". Passed rather
    /// than read off a career, because the exhibition sandbox has a show and no career.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public DateOnly Today { get; set; }
}
